<h2>1. Executive Summary</h2>
<p>Traditional motor insurance claim processing relies heavily on manual paperwork, fragmented communication channels, and physical surveyor inspections. This results in lengthy settlement cycles (typically 14 to 21 business days), high operational overhead, and elevated human error rates. Furthermore, software deployment for such enterprise financial systems is frequently plagued by manual release bottlenecks, configuration drift between staging and production, and prolonged recovery times during unexpected application downtime.</p>
<p>This project addresses these challenges by developing a full-stack, enterprise-grade <b>Motor Insurance Claim Portal</b> coupled with a fully automated, production-ready <b>DevOps CI/CD Pipeline</b>. The solution automates the software development lifecycle from source control commits through automated quality gating, containerization, and configuration management to zero-downtime deployment.</p>
<h3>Key Engineering Accomplishments:</h3>
<ul><li><b>Application Architecture</b>: Modular 3-tier Spring Boot 3.3.4 application running on Java 21 LTS with Thymeleaf dynamic presentation, Bean Validation 3.0, and Spring Data JPA.</li></ul>
<ul><li><b>Continuous Integration</b>: Declarative Jenkins pipeline enforcing strict automated validation, unit testing (Mockito), integration testing (Spring MockMvc), and end-to-end headless browser testing (Selenium WebDriver 4).</li></ul>
<ul><li><b>Quality Defect Gating</b>: Automated gating logic in Jenkins that halts pipeline execution if unreviewed claims or critical defects exceed defined thresholds.</li></ul>
<ul><li><b>Containerization</b>: Multi-stage Docker packaging utilizing Alpine JRE runtime and an unprivileged user (<code>claimuser</code>), slashing image footprint from &gt;650MB to ~180MB.</li></ul>
<ul><li><b>Infrastructure as Code</b>: Modular Ansible role (<code>claim_portal</code>) ensuring idempotent deployment (<code>changed=0</code>) and a systemd-managed service wrapper.</li></ul>
<ul><li><b>Disaster Recovery</b>: Automated health-probe validation (<code>/actuator/health</code>) linked to an automated rollback playbook delivering a Mean Time to Recovery (<b>MTTR</b>) of under 10 seconds.</li></ul>
<hr/>
<h2>Task 1: Problem Definition, Business Scope &amp; Stakeholder Analysis</h2>
<h3>1.1 Problem Statement</h3>
<p>Motor insurance customers face prolonged delays when filing physical or semi-digital claims following vehicle accidents. Manual data entry, physical verification bottlenecks, and siloed reviewer workflows create severe backlogs. From an IT operations perspective, previous claim platforms were deployed via manual JAR copying and server configuration, leading to environmental inconsistencies, runtime crashes, and unmonitored production outages.</p>
<h3>1.2 DevOps Objectives</h3>
<ol><li>Build a responsive, self-service web application allowing policyholders to submit claims and track adjudication progress in real time.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Automate build, test, packaging, and deployment cycles using an opinionated CI/CD pipeline.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Eliminate manual server configuration errors through version-controlled Infrastructure as Code (IaC).</li></ol>
<ol><li>Establish automated monitoring and rapid rollback mechanisms to maintain service availability above 99.9%.</li></ol>
<h3>1.3 Stakeholder Matrix</h3>
<table>
<tr>
<th>Stakeholder Group</th>
<th>Primary Needs &amp; Pain Points</th>
<th>DevOps Solution Delivered</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><b>Policyholder (Customer)</b></td>
<td>Needs quick claim registration, clear required fields, instant reference ID, and transparent status tracking.</td>
<td>Self-service web portal with regex input validation and visual 4-stage tracking stepper.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><b>Claim Surveyor / Reviewer</b></td>
<td>Needs central adjudication dashboard to inspect claims, review estimates, approve/reject, and record remarks.</td>
<td>Reviewer workbench dashboard with real-time status updates and action modals.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><b>DevOps / Release Engineer</b></td>
<td>Needs reliable, reproducible build and deployment mechanisms without manual SSH/file-transfer operations.</td>
<td>Jenkins declarative pipeline, multi-stage Docker containers, and Ansible playbooks.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><b>IT Operations &amp; QA Team</b></td>
<td>Needs automated smoke and regression testing before releases, plus rapid incident recovery during outages.</td>
<td>Headless Selenium test suite, automated quality gates, and automated rollback scripts.</td>
</tr>
</table>
<h3>1.4 Scope Boundary Matrix</h3>
<ul><li><b>In Scope (MVP Deliverables)</b>:</li></ul>
<ul><li>Online claim submission form with client-side and server-side validation.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Unique claim reference generation (<code>CLM-YYYY-XXXXX</code>).</li></ul>
<ul><li>Surveyor adjudication dashboard with status transitions (<code>SUBMITTED</code>, <code>UNDER_REVIEW</code>, <code>APPROVED</code>, <code>REJECTED</code>, <code>SETTLED</code>).</li></ul>
<ul><li>Real-time claim status tracking interface.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Comprehensive 14-task DevOps toolchain (Git, Jenkins, Selenium, Docker, Ansible, Puppet).</li></ul>
<ul><li><b>Out of Scope (Post-MVP)</b>:</li></ul>
<ul><li>Third-party bank payment gateway integration for direct claim fund disbursement.</li></ul>
<ul><li>OCR automated parsing of physical accident repair invoices.</li></ul>
<pre><code>┘</code></pre>
