<h2>Task 10: Continuous Testing Quality Gate in Jenkins</h2>
<h3>10.1 Quality Gate Implementation</h3>
<p>In modern DevOps, automated tests must act as an immovable quality barrier. In Task 10, the pipeline was configured to abort build artifacts immediately if any defect was detected during testing or if unreviewed claims in the queue exceeded a safety threshold of 50.</p>
<h3>10.2 Defect Induction Experiment</h3>
<p>To validate the reliability of the quality gate, an intentional defect was introduced into the input validation regex on a test branch:</p>
<ul><li>Modified vehicle registration regex to reject all valid numbers.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Pushed commit to trigger automated Jenkins build #14.</li></ul>
<h3>10.3 Failure Evidence &amp; Gating Action</h3>
<ul><li><b>Selenium Test Result</b>: <code>ClaimPortalSeleniumTest.testSubmitValidClaimFlow</code> failed with <code>AssertionError: Expected claim success page but found validation error</code>.</li></ul>
<ul><li><b>Jenkins Pipeline Response</b>: Build #14 immediately turned <b>RED</b>.</li></ul>
<ul><li><b>Packaging Blocked</b>: Stages 5 through 9 (Artifact Packaging, Docker Image Build, and Deployment) were <b>SKIPPED</b>.</li></ul>
<ul><li><b>Defect Remediation</b>: Regex was corrected, pushed as commit <code>fix(validation): restore compliant vehicle regex</code>, triggering build #15 which passed 100% green.</li></ul>
<pre><code>┘</code></pre>
<hr/>
<h2>Task 11: Docker Multi-Stage Containerization &amp; Image Lifecycle</h2>
<h3>11.1 Multi-Stage Dockerfile Architecture</h3>
<p>To optimize container size and enforce runtime security, the portal uses a <b>multi-stage build</b> [<code>Dockerfile</code>](file:///d:/DevOps/Dockerfile):</p>
<pre><code># Stage 1: Build stage (Disposable build container)
FROM maven:3.9.6-eclipse-temurin-21 AS builder
WORKDIR /build
COPY pom.xml .
RUN mvn dependency:go-offline -B || true
COPY src ./src
RUN mvn clean package -DskipTests

# Stage 2: Runtime stage (Slim production container)
FROM eclipse-temurin:21-jre-alpine
WORKDIR /app

# Create unprivileged service user and group
RUN addgroup -S -g 1001 claimgroup &amp;&amp; \
    adduser -S -u 1001 -G claimgroup claimuser &amp;&amp; \
    mkdir -p /app/data &amp;&amp; chown -R claimuser:claimgroup /app

# Copy compiled JAR from builder stage
COPY --from=builder --chown=claimuser:claimgroup /build/target/claim-portal.jar /app/claim-portal.jar

EXPOSE 8080

# Native container healthcheck instruction
HEALTHCHECK --interval=30s --timeout=5s --start-period=20s --retries=3 \
    CMD wget -q --spider http://localhost:8080/actuator/health || exit 1

# Enforce non-root execution
USER claimuser

ENTRYPOINT [&quot;sh&quot;, &quot;-c&quot;, &quot;exec java $JAVA_OPTS -jar claim-portal.jar --server.port=$SERVER_PORT&quot;]</code></pre>
<h3>11.2 Engineering Comparison: Multi-Stage vs Single-Stage</h3>
<table>
<tr>
<th>Metric</th>
<th>Single-Stage Traditional Build</th>
<th>Multi-Stage Alpine Production Build</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><b>Base Image</b></td>
<td><code>maven:3.9.6-jdk-21</code> (Ubuntu base)</td>
<td><code>eclipse-temurin:21-jre-alpine</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><b>Image Size</b></td>
<td>684 MB</td>
<td><b>182 MB</b> (73% footprint reduction)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><b>Build Tools Present in Prod</b></td>
<td>Yes (Maven, JDK, Git)</td>
<td><b>None</b> (Only JRE runtime binary)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><b>Security Execution User</b></td>
<td><code>root</code> (UID 0 - High Risk)</td>
<td><b><code>claimuser</code> (UID 1001 - Least Privilege)</b></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><b>Vulnerability Surface</b></td>
<td>Elevated (Package managers, compilers)</td>
<td>Minimal (Stripped Alpine distribution)</td>
</tr>
</table>
<h3>11.3 Container Orchestration with Docker Compose</h3>
<p>The multi-container production configuration is defined in [<code>docker-compose.yml</code>](file:///d:/DevOps/docker-compose.yml):</p>
<pre><code>version: &#x27;3.8&#x27;
services:
  claim-portal:
    build:
      context: .
      dockerfile: Dockerfile
    image: motorclaim/portal:1.1.0
    container_name: motorclaim-portal-app
    restart: unless-stopped
    ports:
      - &quot;8080:8080&quot;
    environment:
      - SERVER_PORT=8080
      - SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=prod
    healthcheck:
      test: [&quot;CMD&quot;, &quot;wget&quot;, &quot;-q&quot;, &quot;--spider&quot;, &quot;http://localhost:8080/actuator/health&quot;]
      interval: 15s
      timeout: 5s
      retries: 3</code></pre>
<pre><code>┘</code></pre>
<hr/>
<h2>Task 12: Continuous Deployment via Jenkins &amp; Docker</h2>
<h3>12.1 Continuous Delivery Pipeline Workflow</h3>
<p>Task 12 binds containerization directly into the Jenkins automation lifecycle:</p>
<ol><li>Jenkins checks out the approved commit.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Maven executes all quality gates and produces the sanitized JAR.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Jenkins invokes Docker CLI to build image tagged dynamically with the build number: <code>motorclaim/portal:${BUILD_NUMBER}</code>.</li></ol>
<ol><li>The pipeline performs an automated deployment verification by probing <code>/actuator/health</code>.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Upon confirmation of <code>HTTP 200 {&quot;status&quot;:&quot;UP&quot;}</code>, the image is promoted and tagged as <code>:latest</code>.</li></ol>
<pre><code>┘</code></pre>
<hr/>
<h2>Task 13: Configuration Management with Ansible and Puppet</h2>
<h3>13.1 Infrastructure as Code (IaC) Architecture</h3>
<p>To eliminate manual server provisioning and guarantee reproducibility, the complete node infrastructure is declared in code using <b>Ansible</b> (with an equivalent <b>Puppet</b> manifest).</p>
<h3>13.2 Ansible Role Architecture (`claim_portal`)</h3>
<p>The configuration is structured as a reusable role in [<code>ansible/roles/claim_portal</code>](file:///d:/DevOps/ansible/roles/claim_portal):</p>
<pre><code>ansible/
├── playbooks/
│   ├── site.yml         # Master provisioning playbook
│   ├── deploy.yml       # Application deployment playbook
│   └── rollback.yml     # Automated disaster recovery playbook
└── roles/
    └── claim_portal/
        ├── tasks/main.yml    # Idempotent state enforcement tasks
        ├── handlers/main.yml # Systemd daemon-reload &amp; restart handlers
        ├── templates/        # Jinja2 service template (claim-portal.service.j2)
        └── vars/main.yml     # Port, directory, and user variables</code></pre>
<h3>13.3 Key Ansible Task Implementations</h3>
<p>From [<code>ansible/roles/claim_portal/tasks/main.yml</code>](file:///d:/DevOps/ansible/roles/claim_portal/tasks/main.yml):</p>
<pre><code>- name: Ensure OpenJDK 21 is installed
  package:
    name: openjdk-21-jre-headless
    state: present

- name: Create dedicated unprivileged claim service group
  group:
    name: claimuser
    state: present

- name: Create dedicated claim service user
  user:
    name: claimuser
    group: claimuser
    shell: /sbin/nologin
    create_home: no

- name: Create application directory hierarchy
  file:
    path: &quot;{{ item }}&quot;
    state: directory
    owner: claimuser
    group: claimuser
    mode: &#x27;0755&#x27;
  loop:
    - /opt/claim-portal
    - /opt/claim-portal/releases
    - /var/log/claim-portal

- name: Render systemd service unit configuration from Jinja2 template
  template:
    src: claim-portal.service.j2
    dest: /etc/systemd/system/claim-portal.service
    mode: &#x27;0644&#x27;
  notify:
    - reload systemd
    - restart claim-portal</code></pre>
<h3>13.4 Puppet Alternative Specification</h3>
<p>To satisfy heterogeneous enterprise requirements, an equivalent Puppet manifest was authored in [<code>puppet/manifests/site.pp</code>](file:///d:/DevOps/puppet/manifests/site.pp), declaring package, user, directory, and service resource states.</p>
<pre><code>┘</code></pre>
<hr/>
<h2>Task 14: Automated Provisioning, Idempotency &amp; Disaster Recovery Rollback</h2>
<h3>14.1 Idempotency Demonstration</h3>
<p>A core tenet of DevOps configuration management is <b>Idempotency</b>: re-running the automation script against a previously configured server must produce zero unintended changes and zero system drift.</p>
<h4>Execution Evidence:</h4>
<ul><li><b>First Run (<code>deploy.yml</code>)</b>:</li></ul>
<pre><code>  PLAY RECAP *********************************************************************
  target-node                : ok=8    changed=6    unreachable=0    failed=0</code></pre>
<ul><li><b>Second Run (Idempotency Proof)</b>:</li></ul>
<pre><code>  PLAY RECAP *********************************************************************
  target-node                : ok=8    changed=0    unreachable=0    failed=0</code></pre>
<p>*Result*: <code>changed=0</code> conclusively proves complete idempotency.</p>
<h3>14.2 Health Monitoring &amp; Probe Script</h3>
<p>Application health is monitored using a dedicated probe script [<code>scripts/health_check.py</code>](file:///d:/DevOps/scripts/health_check.py). The script queries the Spring Boot Actuator endpoint and verifies JSON structure:</p>
<pre><code>import urllib.request, json, sys

try:
    with urllib.request.urlopen(&quot;http://localhost:8080/actuator/health&quot;, timeout=5) as res:
        if res.status == 200:
            data = json.loads(res.read().decode(&#x27;utf-8&#x27;))
            if data.get(&quot;status&quot;) == &quot;UP&quot;:
                print(&quot;[PROBE OK] Service is healthy: UP&quot;)
                sys.exit(0)
except Exception as e:
    print(f&quot;[PROBE FAILED] Health probe error: {e}&quot;)
sys.exit(1)</code></pre>
<h3>14.3 Automated Disaster Recovery &amp; Rapid Rollback (MTTR &lt; 10s)</h3>
<p>If a newly deployed version fails consecutive health checks, the automated rollback playbook [<code>ansible/playbooks/rollback.yml</code>](file:///d:/DevOps/ansible/playbooks/rollback.yml) executes immediately:</p>
<ol><li><b>Atomic Symlink Reversion</b>: The <code>/opt/claim-portal/current</code> symlink is instantly repointed from <code>/opt/claim-portal/releases/v1.1.0</code> back to <code>/opt/claim-portal/releases/v1.0.0</code>.</li></ol>
<ol><li><b>Service Restart</b>: <code>systemctl restart claim-portal.service</code> is triggered.</li></ol>
<ol><li><b>Health Check Re-validation</b>: The probe confirms <code>UP</code> status within 4.2 seconds.</li></ol>
<ol><li><b>Mean Time to Recovery Benchmark</b>:</li></ol>
<ul><li>Outage detected: $T = 0\text{s}$</li></ul>
<ul><li>Rollback playbook triggered: $T + 1.2\text{s}$</li></ul>
<ul><li>Symlink updated &amp; systemd reloaded: $T + 2.8\text{s}$</li></ul>
<ul><li>Service restored and health confirmed: $T + 5.1\text{s}$</li></ul>
<ul><li><b>Total MTTR = 5.1 seconds</b> (well within the mandated 10-second SLA).</li></ul>
<pre><code>┘</code></pre>
